window.GUIDE={"slug":"classical-myth-v2","name":"Classical Myth in Art","base":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto","img":"/study/art-history/classical-myth/img/","parts":[{"h":1,"title":"The Gods of Olympus","wk":1,"section":"Gods and stories"},{"h":2,"title":"Titans, Giants and the First Mortals","wk":1,"section":"Gods and stories"},{"h":3,"title":"Love, Desire and Transformation","wk":1,"section":"Gods and stories"},{"h":4,"title":"Wine, Harvest and the Underworld","wk":1,"section":"Gods and stories"},{"h":5,"title":"Heroes","wk":2,"section":"Heroes and Troy"},{"h":6,"title":"Troy and the Odyssey","wk":2,"section":"Heroes and Troy"},{"h":7,"title":"Interpreting Myth","wk":3,"section":"Reading myth"}],"items":[{"id":"start","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/start.html","h":0,"kind":"intro","label":"About this site","title":"About this site","artist":"","thumb":null,"text":"About this site"},{"id":"h1-0","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/part-1.html","h":1,"kind":"overview","label":"Overview","title":"The Gods of Olympus","artist":"","thumb":"h1_aphrodite","text":"The Gods of Olympus · Who the gods are, and how artists made each one recognizable · The Greeks pictured their gods as a family of twelve living on Mount Olympus, and the Romans took them over under Latin names. Because the gods look human, artists marked each one with a fixed set of objects, animals and poses, so a viewer could name the god at a glance. · Ancient statues set the standard types that later painters copied. · Renaissance and Baroque artists revived the gods for princely palaces and collections. · Learn the attributes here and the rest of the guide becomes easier to read. · Attribute · An object, animal or feature that identifies a figure, such as Zeus’s thunderbolt or Athena’s owl and aegis. · Sandro Botticelli · The Birth of Venus · c. 1485"},{"id":"h1_zeus","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h1_zeus.html","h":1,"kind":"work","label":"Jupiter of Smyrna","title":"Jupiter of Smyrna","artist":"Roman, sculptor unknown","thumb":"h1_zeus","text":"Jupiter of Smyrna · Roman, sculptor unknown · 2nd century CE · Marble, 270 cm high · Louvre, Paris · An over-life-size Roman marble of the king of the gods, carved in the province of Asia and possibly copied from a Greek original about three centuries older. Much of what you see was rebuilt for Louis XIV. · Recorded at Smyrna (modern İzmir) in 1680, owned by a Dutch merchant, and bought for Louis XIV. · Pierre Granier restored it in 1686: the shoulder, raised arm and thunderbolt, nose, feet and base are his. · Stood on the Allée Royale at Versailles from 1687; moved to the Louvre after the Revolution. · Look for the deep drill work in the hair and beard, typical of 2nd-century Roman carving. · Since the thunderbolt is modern, the Louvre also lists Asclepius, god of healing, as a possible identity. · Himation · A large rectangular cloak worn by Greek men, here draped to leave the chest bare. · Zeus (Roman Jupiter) is king of the gods and god of sky and thunder, who took power by overthrowing his father Cronus. Sculptors showed him as a mature, bearded man, bare-chested, with scepter and thunderbolt."},{"id":"h1_hera","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h1_hera.html","h":1,"kind":"work","label":"Juno and Argus","title":"Juno and Argus","artist":"Peter Paul Rubens","thumb":"h1_hera","text":"Juno and Argus · Peter Paul Rubens · c. 1610 · Oil on canvas, 249 × 296 cm · Wallraf-Richartz-Museum, Cologne · Painted soon after Rubens came home to Antwerp from eight years in Italy, this big canvas shows Juno turning a dead guardian’s eyes into the peacock’s tail. It is a showcase for the Venetian colour Rubens had just studied. · Juno lifts the eyes from the headless body of Argus, helped by Iris, goddess of the rainbow, and a crowd of putti. · A rainbow runs through the centre; the museum notes the picture is built on blue, yellow and red. · Its design echoes the title page Rubens drew for a 1613 book on optics. · Given to the museum in 1894 by a group of Cologne art lovers; the original patron is not known. · Hera (Roman Juno) is queen of the gods and goddess of marriage. She set hundred-eyed Argus to guard Io, one of Zeus’s loves; when Hermes killed him, she placed his eyes on her sacred bird, the peacock."},{"id":"h1_poseidon","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h1_poseidon.html","h":1,"kind":"work","label":"Bronze God from Cape Artemision","title":"Bronze God from Cape Artemision","artist":"Greek, sculptor unknown","thumb":"h1_poseidon","text":"Bronze God from Cape Artemision · Greek, sculptor unknown · c. 460 BCE · Bronze, 209 cm high · National Archaeological Museum, Athens · One of the very few full-size Greek bronzes to survive, raised from the sea off northern Euboea. Most ancient bronzes were melted down; this one lasted because it sank. · Fishermen netted an arm around 1926; the nearly complete statue was brought up in September 1928. · The museum groups it with bronzes lost when Roman ships carrying art from Greece were wrecked. · Look at the arms: stretched to balance a throw, with a span (210 cm) slightly wider than the figure is tall. · The weapon in the right hand is lost, so it may be Zeus with a thunderbolt or Poseidon with a trident. · Poseidon (Roman Neptune), brother of Zeus, rules the sea, earthquakes and horses. The figure’s identity is still argued because only the missing weapon would settle it."},{"id":"h6_varvakeion","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h6_varvakeion.html","h":1,"kind":"work","label":"Varvakeion Athena","title":"Varvakeion Athena","artist":"Roman, after Pheidias","thumb":"h6_varvakeion","text":"Varvakeion Athena · Roman, after Pheidias · 200–250 CE · Marble with traces of paint, about 1 m high · National Archaeological Museum, Athens · A small Roman copy of the most famous statue in Athens, Pheidias’s gold-and-ivory Athena Parthenos, which stood about 12 m high inside the Parthenon. The original is lost, so copies like this are the best evidence of how it looked. · Found in Athens near the Varvakeion, in the ruins of a Roman house. · Traces of paint stood in for the gold and ivory of the original. · Its details match the ancient description by Pausanias: Victory in one hand, a shield at her feet and a serpent beside it. · Cambridge’s cast collection describes such statuettes as souvenirs of the famous original. · Athena (Roman Minerva) is goddess of wisdom, crafts and strategic war, born fully armed from Zeus’s head. Athens, her city, built the Parthenon in her honour."},{"id":"h1_athena","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h1_athena.html","h":1,"kind":"work","label":"Minerva Expelling the Vices from the Garden of Virtue","title":"Minerva Expelling the Vices from the Garden of Virtue","artist":"Andrea Mantegna","thumb":"h1_athena","text":"Minerva Expelling the Vices from the Garden of Virtue · Andrea Mantegna · completed 1502 · Tempera on canvas, 160 × 192 cm · Louvre, Paris · Made for Isabella d’Este’s studiolo in Mantua, a small private study she filled with paintings on moral themes. Mantegna’s crowded allegory shows Minerva clearing a garden of grotesque Vices. · One of five canvases for the room; it hung opposite Mantegna’s Parnassus. · Look for the Vices in flight, among them armless Sloth and Ignorance carried by Ingratitude. · Hidden images: a tree in human form and a face in a cloud. · Inscriptions in several alphabets label the figures. · Later owned by Cardinal Richelieu; entered the French state collections in 1801. · As goddess of wisdom, Minerva was a natural champion of virtue over vice. Here the myth is used as a moral lesson rather than a story."},{"id":"h1_apollo","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h1_apollo.html","h":1,"kind":"work","label":"Apollo Belvedere","title":"Apollo Belvedere","artist":"Roman, after a Greek bronze","thumb":"h1_apollo","text":"Apollo Belvedere · Roman, after a Greek bronze · early 2nd century CE · Marble · Vatican Museums, Vatican City · A Roman copy of a Greek bronze of about 330 BCE, probably by Leochares. Found in Rome in 1489, it became for three centuries the model of ideal male beauty. · Cardinal Giuliano della Rovere owned it and, as Pope Julius II, set it in the Vatican’s Belvedere court by 1508. · Montorsoli rebuilt the missing arm and hand in 1532–33; the left hand is now a cast of an ancient fragment found at Baia. · Look at the pose: the god has just loosed an arrow; the bow in his left hand is lost. · Taken to Paris under Napoleon and returned in 1816. · Chlamys · A short cloak pinned at the shoulder, here hanging from Apollo’s arm. · Apollo, son of Zeus, is god of light, music, prophecy and archery. He is always young and beardless, often with bow, lyre or laurel."},{"id":"h1_artemis","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h1_artemis.html","h":1,"kind":"work","label":"Diana and Her Nymphs","title":"Diana and Her Nymphs","artist":"Johannes Vermeer","thumb":"h1_artemis","text":"Diana and Her Nymphs · Johannes Vermeer · c. 1653–54 · Oil on canvas, 97.8 × 104.6 cm · Mauritshuis, The Hague · One of Vermeer’s earliest paintings, made just after he joined the Delft painters’ guild and years before his quiet interiors. It shows the huntress at rest at night, attended by her nymphs. · Long sold as a work by Nicolaes Maes; in 1885 the “N.M.” monogram proved a forgery over Vermeer’s signature. · Look for the small crescent moon on Diana’s head and a nymph washing her feet. · A later blue sky was covered in 1999–2000 to bring back the night setting. · The canvas was cut down on the right, cropping the kneeling figure at the edge. · Artemis (Roman Diana), Apollo’s twin, is goddess of the hunt, wild places and the moon, and a virgin protector of young women."},{"id":"h1_ares","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h1_ares.html","h":1,"kind":"work","label":"Mars","title":"Mars","artist":"Diego Velázquez","thumb":"h1_ares","text":"Mars · Diego Velázquez · c. 1638 · Oil on canvas, 179 × 95 cm · Museo del Prado, Madrid · Velázquez turns the god of war into a weary man sitting on a bed, naked except for his helmet. The painting was recorded in Philip IV’s hunting lodge, the Torre de la Parada. · Recorded at the Torre de la Parada in 1701–03, beside Velázquez’s Aesop and Menippus of similar size. · Look for armour, shield and sword dropped at the foot of the bed and the baton resting on the floor. · The Prado traces the pose to the ancient Ludovisi Ares and to Michelangelo’s Pensieroso. · Loose brushwork builds the body with soft outlines. · Ares (Roman Mars) is the god of war, admired by the Romans more than the Greeks. His affair with Venus was a favourite subject for showing love disarming war."},{"id":"h1_aphrodite","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h1_aphrodite.html","h":1,"kind":"work","label":"The Birth of Venus","title":"The Birth of Venus","artist":"Sandro Botticelli","thumb":"h1_aphrodite","text":"The Birth of Venus · Sandro Botticelli · c. 1485 · Tempera on canvas, 172.5 × 278.5 cm · Uffizi Gallery, Florence · One of the first large paintings of a pagan goddess since antiquity, very probably made for a member of the Medici family. It is painted on canvas, a support then used for decoration in great houses. · First recorded in 1550 by Vasari at the Medici villa of Castello. · Venus’s pose comes from ancient statues; gold highlights run through her hair. · The two winds at left are modelled on an ancient gem owned by Lorenzo the Magnificent. · Orange trees, a Medici emblem, and roses blown on the wind. · The cloaked woman is sometimes identified as the Hora of spring or one of the Graces. · Aphrodite (Roman Venus) is the goddess of love and beauty. In Hesiod she rises full-grown from the sea foam and comes ashore on Cyprus."},{"id":"h1_hephaestus","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h1_hephaestus.html","h":1,"kind":"work","label":"Vulcan’s Forge","title":"Vulcan’s Forge","artist":"Diego Velázquez","thumb":"h1_hephaestus","text":"Vulcan’s Forge · Diego Velázquez · 1630 · Oil on canvas, 223 × 290 cm · Museo del Prado, Madrid · Painted in Rome during Velázquez’s first Italian trip, without a commission, then sold by the artist to Philip IV in 1634. It shows the moment the smith god hears bad news. · Apollo, in orange with a laurel wreath and a halo of light, tells Vulcan that his wife Venus is Mars’s lover. · The armour on the anvil is being made for Mars. · Look at whole bodies freezing mid-movement, not only faces, to show the shock. · Velázquez’s first large work on a lead-white ground, giving it a lighter tone. · Hephaestus (Roman Vulcan) is the lame smith god, maker of the gods’ weapons and married to Aphrodite. Apollo, who sees everything, reveals her affair."},{"id":"h1_hermes","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h1_hermes.html","h":1,"kind":"work","label":"Hermes and the Infant Dionysus","title":"Hermes and the Infant Dionysus","artist":"Attributed to Praxiteles","thumb":"h1_hermes","text":"Hermes and the Infant Dionysus · Attributed to Praxiteles · 4th century BCE or later (disputed) · Parian marble, about 2.1 m high · Archaeological Museum of Olympia · Found in 1877 in the Temple of Hera at Olympia, where the ancient travel writer Pausanias recorded a Hermes by Praxiteles. Whether this is that original, a later work or a copy is still debated. · Excavated in May 1877; the right foot turned up in later digs. · Pausanias is the only ancient source to mention it. · Hermes holds the baby Dionysus on his left arm; the lost right hand probably dangled grapes. · Proposed dates run from the mid-4th century BCE to about 100 BCE. · Hermes (Roman Mercury) is the messenger god and guide of travellers. Zeus gave him the infant Dionysus to carry to safety, away from Hera’s jealousy."},{"id":"h2-0","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/part-2.html","h":2,"kind":"overview","label":"Overview","title":"Titans, Giants and the First Mortals","artist":"","thumb":"h3_phaeton","text":"Titans, Giants and the First Mortals · Wars in heaven, and the making and punishing of humankind · Greek creation stories begin with violence: the Titans overthrow the first gods, and Zeus and the Olympians overthrow the Titans and then defeat the Giants. Mortals arrive in the middle of this struggle, made by Prometheus and burdened by Pandora. · Battles of gods against monsters gave artists a licence for twisting, colossal bodies. · Prometheus and Pandora explain why humans have fire, and why they suffer. · Later painters linked Pandora with Eve and the Flood with Noah. · Gigantomachy · The war between the Olympian gods and the Giants, a favourite subject for temple sculpture. · Titanomachy · The earlier war in which Zeus and his siblings defeated the Titans. · Peter Paul Rubens · The Fall of Phaeton · c. 1604–08"},{"id":"h3_giulio","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h3_giulio.html","h":2,"kind":"work","label":"Sala dei Giganti (Room of the Giants)","title":"Sala dei Giganti (Room of the Giants)","artist":"Giulio Romano (design) and Rinaldo Mantovano","thumb":"h3_giulio","text":"Sala dei Giganti (Room of the Giants) · Giulio Romano (design) and Rinaldo Mantovano · 1532–35 · Fresco on walls and vault · Palazzo Te, Mantua · A room in Federico II Gonzaga’s pleasure villa where fresco covers every wall and the vault without a break, so visitors stand inside the collapse of the Giants. Giulio designed it; his assistant Rinaldo Mantovano painted much of it. · Look up: Jupiter hurls thunderbolts from the vault, ringed by alarmed gods. · On the walls Giants are crushed by falling columns, rocks and a collapsing temple. · Monkeys on one wall come from a period misreading of Ovid. · Scaffolding came down for Emperor Charles V’s visit in 1532 while parts were unfinished; the room is often read as flattering him. · Restored in the 1780s and again in 1988. · The Giants, children of Earth, attacked Olympus and were destroyed by Jupiter’s thunderbolts. Ovid’s version of the story is the painting’s source."},{"id":"h3_titans","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h3_titans.html","h":2,"kind":"work","label":"The Fall of the Titans","title":"The Fall of the Titans","artist":"Cornelis van Haarlem","thumb":"h3_titans","text":"The Fall of the Titans · Cornelis van Haarlem · 1588–90 · Oil on canvas, 239 × 307 cm · SMK, National Gallery of Denmark, Copenhagen · A Dutch Mannerist showpiece of tumbling nude bodies by a Haarlem painter whose circle was among the first to work from nude models. No commission is recorded. · Bought in 1621 by King Christian IV of Denmark. · Look at the bodies falling from light into darkness and piling up, mouths open and hands raised. · Foreground figures frame the ones behind and pull the eye deep into space. · Butterflies and insects cover the figures’ groins, which the museum links to Hell. · Long in storage; restored and rehung in 1988. · The Titans, the generation before the Olympians, were defeated by Zeus and thrown down into Tartarus, the deepest part of the underworld."},{"id":"h3_phaeton","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h3_phaeton.html","h":2,"kind":"work","label":"The Fall of Phaeton","title":"The Fall of Phaeton","artist":"Peter Paul Rubens","thumb":"h3_phaeton","text":"The Fall of Phaeton · Peter Paul Rubens · c. 1604–08 · Oil on canvas, 98.4 × 131.2 cm · National Gallery of Art, Washington · Painted by the young Rubens in Italy and reworked over several years; painted-over reins and straps still show through. The picture catches the instant the sun’s chariot breaks apart. · Phaeton falls headfirst, wearing only a red sash, as the four horses scatter. · The butterfly-winged women at left are the Horae, the seasons, reacting in terror. · At lower right the earth catches fire; Jupiter’s thunderbolt strikes from outside the frame. · Probably the “Phaeton by Rubens” listed in an Antwerp inventory of 1657; bought by the gallery in 1990. · Phaeton begged his father, the sun god, to let him drive the sun’s chariot for a day. He lost control, scorched the earth, and was struck down by Jupiter."},{"id":"h3_perg_c","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h3_perg_c.html","h":2,"kind":"work","label":"Pergamon Altar: Athena Fighting the Giants","title":"Pergamon Altar: Athena Fighting the Giants","artist":"Pergamene workshop","thumb":"h3_perg_c","text":"Pergamon Altar: Athena Fighting the Giants · Pergamene workshop · c. 175–150 BCE · Marble high relief · Pergamonmuseum, Berlin · Part of the Great Frieze, more than 100 m of high relief that wrapped the monumental altar at Pergamon, built under King Eumenes II. It is the largest surviving telling of the war against the Giants. · Athena grabs the winged Giant Alkyoneus by the hair while her serpent bites his chest. · Earth (Ge) rises from the ground pleading for her son; Victory flies in to crown Athena. · Scholars link the Giants to enemies of the Attalid kings, such as the Gauls. · In Berlin since 1930; the altar hall is closed for renovation and due to reopen in spring 2027. · In the Gigantomachy the Olympians, helped by Heracles, defeated the Giants. Athena’s victory over Alkyoneus was one of its best-known episodes."},{"id":"h3_typhon","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h3_typhon.html","h":2,"kind":"work","label":"Hydria with Zeus Fighting Typhon","title":"Hydria with Zeus Fighting Typhon","artist":"Chalcidian workshop","thumb":"h3_typhon","text":"Hydria with Zeus Fighting Typhon · Chalcidian workshop · c. 540–530 BCE · Black-figure terracotta, 46 cm high · Staatliche Antikensammlungen, Munich · A water jar decorated in the black-figure technique, from the Chalcidian workshops whose home is still debated. One side shows Zeus’s last great battle. · Zeus strides in with a thunderbolt against Typhon. · Typhon is painted with wings and serpent legs; the hundred heads of the poems are not shown. · The other side shows the hero Peleus wrestling the huntress Atalanta. · Where it was found is not recorded in the sources checked. · Black-figure · A vase technique in which figures are painted in black slip and details scratched through to the clay. · Hydria · A three-handled jar for carrying water. · Typhon, the last child of Earth, challenged Zeus for rule of the world. Zeus defeated him and buried him beneath a mountain, often said to be Etna."},{"id":"h4_piero","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h4_piero.html","h":2,"kind":"work","label":"The Myth of Prometheus","title":"The Myth of Prometheus","artist":"Piero di Cosimo","thumb":"h4_piero","text":"The Myth of Prometheus · Piero di Cosimo · c. 1510–15 · Oil on poplar panel, 66.4 × 118.2 cm · Alte Pinakothek, Munich · A spalliera panel, a painting set into the panelling or furniture of a room, and the first narrative painting of the Prometheus story in modern times. Its companion panel is in Strasbourg. · Several episodes run across one wide landscape. · At centre stands the statue of a youth Prometheus has made. · Minerva talks with Prometheus and then carries him up to heaven to find the spark of life. · The story continues on the Strasbourg panel: the figure brought to life, the theft of fire and Prometheus bound. · Bought by the museum in 1918. · In one tradition Prometheus, a Titan, shaped the first humans from clay; Minerva helped him. The painter followed Boccaccio’s retelling of the myth."},{"id":"h4_rubens","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h4_rubens.html","h":2,"kind":"work","label":"Prometheus Bound","title":"Prometheus Bound","artist":"Peter Paul Rubens and Frans Snyders","thumb":"h4_rubens","text":"Prometheus Bound · Peter Paul Rubens and Frans Snyders · c. 1611–18 · Oil on canvas, 242.6 × 209.6 cm · Philadelphia Museum of Art · One of the first major works from Rubens’s Antwerp studio, which he thought among his most important pictures. The eagle was painted by his colleague Frans Snyders. · The eagle tears open the chest and digs a talon into one eye, while the other eye stares at the bird. · The tumbling body comes from Titian’s Tityus; the muscles from Michelangelo. · The left arm almost leaves the canvas, pushing the figure toward the viewer. · Look for the torch, sign of the stolen fire. · Bought by the museum in 1950. · For giving fire to humans, Zeus chained Prometheus to a mountain in the Caucasus, where an eagle ate his liver every day. The story is told in the play attributed to Aeschylus."},{"id":"h4_cousin","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h4_cousin.html","h":2,"kind":"work","label":"Eva Prima Pandora","title":"Eva Prima Pandora","artist":"Jean Cousin the Elder","thumb":"h4_cousin","text":"Eva Prima Pandora · Jean Cousin the Elder · after 1549 · Painting on wood panel, 97.5 × 150 cm · Louvre, Paris · A French Renaissance reclining nude by Jean Cousin, who worked in the town of Sens. Its title fuses Eve, the first woman of the Bible, with Pandora, the first woman of Greek myth. · The tablet above her reads “Eva Prima Pandora” (Eve, the first Pandora). · She leans on a skull, a reminder of death. · A serpent and apple point to Eden; a ruined landscape lies behind. · Bought by the Louvre in 1922 from Sens. · Zeus had Pandora made as the first woman to punish humans for Prometheus’s gift. She opened a jar that released every evil, leaving only Hope inside."},{"id":"h4_deluge","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h4_deluge.html","h":2,"kind":"work","label":"The Deluge","title":"The Deluge","artist":"Michelangelo","thumb":"h4_deluge","text":"The Deluge · Michelangelo · c. 1508–09 · Fresco · Sistine Chapel ceiling, Vatican City · One of the nine Genesis scenes Michelangelo painted on the Sistine Chapel ceiling for Pope Julius II, and among the first he finished. It is far more crowded than the scenes he painted later. · People climb a hillside at left to escape the rising water. · A group shelters under a makeshift tent at right; a small boat is about to capsize. · The ark waits in the background. · Julius first wanted apostles on the ceiling; Michelangelo proposed Old Testament scenes. · The Greeks had their own flood story: Zeus drowned a wicked humanity, and only Deucalion and Pyrrha survived to repeople the earth. Renaissance viewers saw it as a pagan echo of Noah."},{"id":"h3-0","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/part-3.html","h":3,"kind":"overview","label":"Overview","title":"Love, Desire and Transformation","artist":"","thumb":"h5_europa","text":"Love, Desire and Transformation · The loves of the gods, and bodies that change shape · Many myths turn on desire: gods pursue mortals, often in disguise, and the chase ends in a change of form. Ovid gathered these stories in the Metamorphoses, and from the Renaissance on they gave painters a respectable reason to paint the nude. · Titian’s poesie for Philip II set the standard for painting these stories. · Gods transform themselves to seduce; mortals are transformed to escape or as punishment. · Many of these paintings were made for private rooms and bedchambers. · Metamorphosis · A change of shape, the theme of Ovid’s poem of about 8 CE and the main source for these paintings. · Poesia · Titian’s word for his mythological paintings for Philip II: painted poems based on Ovid. · Titian · The Rape of Europa · 1559–62"},{"id":"h5_danae","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h5_danae.html","h":3,"kind":"work","label":"Danaë and the Shower of Gold","title":"Danaë and the Shower of Gold","artist":"Titian","thumb":"h5_danae","text":"Danaë and the Shower of Gold · Titian · 1560–65 · Oil on canvas, 129.8 × 181.2 cm · Museo del Prado, Madrid · A new, horizontal version of the Danaë Titian had sent Philip II in 1553, painted with looser handling. The Prado’s catalogue thinks it was made for a private patron who knew Titian, not for the king. · The old nurse catching the gold in her apron sets youth against age, nude against clothed. · Gold pours from the cloud like falling coins. · A small dog on the bed is read by the Prado as a hint of lust. · Kept from 1827 in a restricted room of Madrid’s Academy of San Fernando. · King Acrisius locked his daughter Danaë away after an oracle said her son would kill him. Zeus reached her as a shower of gold, and she bore the hero Perseus."},{"id":"h5_europa","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h5_europa.html","h":3,"kind":"work","label":"The Rape of Europa","title":"The Rape of Europa","artist":"Titian","thumb":"h5_europa","text":"The Rape of Europa · Titian · 1559–62 · Oil on canvas, 178 × 205 cm · Isabella Stewart Gardner Museum, Boston · The sixth and last of Titian’s poesie for Philip II of Spain, shipped from Venice in 1562. It is the only one of the series in America. · Europa clings to the bull’s horn and waves a red veil back to her friends on the shore. · Cupid riding a dolphin repeats her pose; Titian’s signature sits at lower left. · Given to the future Charles I of England in 1623, but it stayed in Madrid when the marriage talks failed. · Isabella Stewart Gardner bought it in 1896; restored in 2018–19. · Zeus took the form of a white bull, charmed the Phoenician princess Europa, and swam off with her to Crete. The continent of Europe was later named after her."},{"id":"h5_ganymede","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h5_ganymede.html","h":3,"kind":"work","label":"The Abduction of Ganymede","title":"The Abduction of Ganymede","artist":"Correggio","thumb":"h5_ganymede","text":"The Abduction of Ganymede · Correggio · c. 1530 · Oil on canvas, 163.5 × 72 cm · Kunsthistorisches Museum, Vienna · Commissioned by Federico II Gonzaga of Mantua as part of a series on Jupiter’s loves, and probably given by him to Emperor Charles V. Its tall, narrow shape matches its companion, Jupiter and Io. · A startled dog at the bottom looks up and leads the eye skyward. · Ganymede turns to face us while the eagle, wings cut off by the edge, carries him off. · Owned by Philip II after 1585 and bought by Emperor Rudolf II in 1605. · Zeus, as an eagle, carried the Trojan prince Ganymede up to Olympus to be cupbearer to the gods."},{"id":"h5_bernini","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h5_bernini.html","h":3,"kind":"work","label":"Apollo and Daphne","title":"Apollo and Daphne","artist":"Piero del Pollaiuolo","thumb":"h5_bernini","text":"Apollo and Daphne · Piero del Pollaiuolo · probably 1470–80 · Oil on wood, 29.5 × 20 cm · National Gallery, London · A tiny panel meant to be held and studied up close, possibly made for Lorenzo de’ Medici, whose emblem used the laurel. It was once thought to come from a piece of furniture. · Daphne’s arms have already become great laurel branches, and one leg a trunk. · Apollo runs in a rich hunting costume picked out with gold. · Behind them is the Arno valley with a distant city, probably Florence. · Still in a 16th-century Italian frame; at the National Gallery since 1876. · Apollo pursued the nymph Daphne, who prayed to escape and was turned into a laurel tree. The laurel became Apollo’s sacred plant."},{"id":"h6_actaeon","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h6_actaeon.html","h":3,"kind":"work","label":"Diana and Actaeon","title":"Diana and Actaeon","artist":"Titian","thumb":"h6_actaeon","text":"Diana and Actaeon · Titian · 1556–59 · Oil on canvas, 184.5 × 202.2 cm · National Gallery, London, and National Galleries of Scotland (shared) · One of Titian’s poesie for Philip II, designed to hang beside Diana and Callisto so the two landscapes ran on into each other. The London and Scottish national galleries bought it together in 2009. · A red curtain is pulled aside as Actaeon bursts in on the bathing goddess. · A stag’s skull on a stone pillar foretells his fate. · Diana, with a crescent in her hair, glares sideways while an attendant draws a veil. · Now shown in turn in London and Edinburgh. · The hunter Actaeon stumbled on Diana bathing. She turned him into a stag, and his own hounds tore him apart."},{"id":"h6_adonis","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h6_adonis.html","h":3,"kind":"work","label":"Venus and Adonis","title":"Venus and Adonis","artist":"Titian","thumb":"h6_adonis","text":"Venus and Adonis · Titian · 1550s–1560s · Oil on canvas, 106.7 × 133.4 cm · Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Titian’s most repeated composition: at least 30 versions by him, his workshop and copyists are known. This is the “two-dog” type, a different design from the version he painted for Philip II. · Venus, seen from behind, clings to Adonis as he leaves for the hunt. · Cupid is awake and frightened; a rainbow and a burst of light fill the sky. · The Met dates it to the 1550s; the National Gallery of Art’s catalogue places it in the 1560s with workshop help. · Venus loved the mortal hunter Adonis and begged him not to go hunting. He was killed by a boar, and the anemone grew from his blood."},{"id":"h6_marsvenus","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h6_marsvenus.html","h":3,"kind":"work","label":"Venus and Mars","title":"Venus and Mars","artist":"Sandro Botticelli","thumb":"h6_marsvenus","text":"Venus and Mars · Sandro Botticelli · c. 1485 · Tempera and oil on wood, 69.2 × 173.4 cm · National Gallery, London · A spalliera, a long panel hung at shoulder height or above, probably made to celebrate a marriage and hung in a bedchamber. Love has disarmed war. · Baby satyrs play with Mars’s armour: one wears his helmet, one sits in his breastplate. · One blows a conch shell in Mars’s ear, and he still sleeps. · Wasps swarm near his head, possibly a pun on the Vespucci family name. · Bought by the National Gallery in 1874. · Venus, though married to Vulcan, was the lover of Mars. Renaissance thinkers read the pair as love calming strife."},{"id":"h6_pygmalion","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h6_pygmalion.html","h":3,"kind":"work","label":"Pygmalion and Galatea","title":"Pygmalion and Galatea","artist":"Jean-Léon Gérôme","thumb":"h6_pygmalion","text":"Pygmalion and Galatea · Jean-Léon Gérôme · c. 1890 · Oil on canvas, 88.9 × 68.6 cm · Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · One of three oil versions Gérôme painted of his own painted marble sculpture of the subject, each showing the statue from a different angle. Here a sculptor paints his sculpture coming alive. · It shows the moment in Ovid when Venus brings the statue to life. · Each of the three paintings views the statue from a different side, as if walking around it. · Gérôme’s marble version is at Hearst Castle, California. · Given to the Met in 1927. · In Ovid, the sculptor Pygmalion fell in love with the ivory statue he had carved, and Venus brought it to life. The name Galatea was given to her by later writers."},{"id":"h6_echo","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h6_echo.html","h":3,"kind":"work","label":"Echo and Narcissus","title":"Echo and Narcissus","artist":"John William Waterhouse","thumb":"h6_echo","text":"Echo and Narcissus · John William Waterhouse · 1903 · Oil on canvas, 109 × 189 cm · Walker Art Gallery, Liverpool · Shown at the Royal Academy in 1903 and bought by the Walker Art Gallery the same year. Waterhouse sets the two figures on opposite banks of a pool. · Narcissus lies on the bank, lost in his own reflection. · Echo watches from across the water, hemmed in and unseen. · The landscape is built from bold, decorative shapes. · The nymph Echo, able only to repeat others’ words, loved Narcissus, who loved only his reflection. She faded to a voice; he wasted away and became a flower."},{"id":"h6_milo","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h6_milo.html","h":3,"kind":"work","label":"Venus de Milo","title":"Venus de Milo","artist":"Hellenistic sculptor","thumb":"h6_milo","text":"Venus de Milo · Hellenistic sculptor · c. 150–100 BCE · Parian marble, 204 cm high · Louvre, Paris · Found by a farmer on the island of Melos in April 1820, bought by the French ambassador, and given to Louis XVIII, who gave it to the Louvre in 1821. It is the most famous Aphrodite to survive from antiquity. · Carved in two blocks joined at the line of the drapery around the hips. · Drill holes once held metal jewellery, such as a bracelet. · The arms were never found; proposals include an apple or a shield used as a mirror. · Found with an inscription from a gymnasium, but where it stood is unknown. · Aphrodite was worshipped across the Greek world. Late Hellenistic sculptors showed her half-draped, a type that begins with Praxiteles’s famous nude Aphrodite of Knidos."},{"id":"h4-0","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/part-4.html","h":4,"kind":"overview","label":"Overview","title":"Wine, Harvest and the Underworld","artist":"","thumb":"h10_patinir","text":"Wine, Harvest and the Underworld · Demeter, Dionysus and the journey to the land of the dead · Two gods of the earth’s cycles, Demeter of the grain and Dionysus of the vine, stand close to the world of the dead. Their myths explain the seasons and promise renewal, while stories of journeys to the underworld test what happens after death. · Persephone’s yearly return from the underworld explains spring. · Dionysus brings wine, ecstasy and theatre, and punishes those who refuse him. · Heroes and poets who visit the underworld bring back warnings for the living. · Underworld · The realm of the dead ruled by Hades (Roman Pluto), reached by crossing the river Styx. · Bacchanal · A painting of the drunken followers of Bacchus, the Roman Dionysus. · Joachim Patinir · Charon Crossing the Styx · 1520–24"},{"id":"h9_bernini","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h9_bernini.html","h":4,"kind":"work","label":"The Abduction of Proserpina","title":"The Abduction of Proserpina","artist":"Rembrandt","thumb":"h9_bernini","text":"The Abduction of Proserpina · Rembrandt · 1631 · Oil on oak panel, 84.4 × 79.5 cm · Gemäldegalerie, Berlin · One of Rembrandt’s small-figure history paintings from around the time he moved from Leiden to Amsterdam, and a rare subject in Dutch art. It was probably in the collection of the Dutch stadholder Frederik Hendrik by 1632. · Proserpina screams and claws at Pluto’s face while her companions grab at her cloak. · Bright blue sky at upper left (the earth) gives way to darkness at lower right (Pluto’s realm). · The blue is costly ultramarine, made from lapis lazuli, and is original. · Technical study shows Rembrandt first painted four horses, later reduced to two. · The design likely depends on a composition by Rubens known from an engraving. · Pluto (Greek Hades) seized Proserpina, daughter of the harvest goddess Ceres, while she gathered flowers, and carried her down to rule as his queen."},{"id":"h1_persephone","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h1_persephone.html","h":4,"kind":"work","label":"The Return of Persephone","title":"The Return of Persephone","artist":"Frederic Leighton","thumb":"h1_persephone","text":"The Return of Persephone · Frederic Leighton · c. 1891 · Oil on canvas, 203 × 152.4 cm · Leeds Art Gallery · A tall Victorian canvas planned through many drawings; a squared-up tracing of 1891 at the Royal Academy matches the finished picture almost exactly. Sir James Kitson bought it and gave it to Leeds. · Hermes lifts Persephone up out of the underworld toward her mother Demeter, at left. · Leighton made separate studies for the heavy drapery. · A 1905 guide counted nineteen preparatory drawings; many survive in British collections. · Published as a photogravure by the Fine Art Society in 1897. · Demeter’s grief for her daughter left the earth barren until Zeus sent Hermes to bring Persephone back. She must return below for part of each year, which is why winter comes."},{"id":"h9_bacchus","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h9_bacchus.html","h":4,"kind":"work","label":"Bacchus","title":"Bacchus","artist":"Caravaggio","thumb":"h9_bacchus","text":"Bacchus · Caravaggio · c. 1598 · Oil on canvas, 95 × 85 cm · Uffizi Gallery, Florence · An early Caravaggio: a young model in a white sheet posing as the wine god, offering a glass. Cardinal del Monte, the painter’s first patron, gave it to the Medici for the wedding of Cosimo II in 1608. · Rediscovered in the Uffizi storerooms in 1913 and attributed to Caravaggio by Roberto Longhi. · The fruit bowl includes a half-rotten apple among pomegranates, quinces, figs and grapes. · Look closely at the carafe: a tiny reflected face, generally read as a self-portrait, was confirmed by infrared study. · Grapes and vine leaves sit in the dark curls; the fingernails are dirty. · Dionysus (Roman Bacchus), son of Zeus and the mortal Semele, is god of wine, release and theatre."},{"id":"h9_midas","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h9_midas.html","h":4,"kind":"work","label":"Midas and Bacchus","title":"Midas and Bacchus","artist":"Nicolas Poussin","thumb":"h9_midas","text":"Midas and Bacchus · Nicolas Poussin · c. 1624 · Oil on canvas, 98.5 × 135 cm · Alte Pinakothek, Munich · An early work from about the time Poussin arrived in Rome in 1624. It shows the start of the story, when Midas tells Bacchus his wish. · Midas makes his request before the wine god. · In the right background Midas washes in the river Pactolus to undo the gift. · Owned by Elector Max Emanuel of Bavaria; it came to the museum from Nymphenburg Palace. · For returning his tutor Silenus, Bacchus granted King Midas a wish. Midas asked that all he touched turn to gold, then found he could not eat, and was cured by bathing in the Pactolus."},{"id":"h9_exekias","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h9_exekias.html","h":4,"kind":"work","label":"Dionysus Cup","title":"Dionysus Cup","artist":"Exekias","thumb":"h9_exekias","text":"Dionysus Cup · Exekias · c. 530 BCE · Black-figure terracotta, 30.5 cm diameter · Staatliche Antikensammlungen, Munich · An Athenian wine cup signed by Exekias, the leading black-figure artist of his day. It was exported to Etruria and found in a tomb at Vulci. · Dionysus reclines in a ship whose mast sprouts a vine with seven bunches of grapes. · Seven dolphins circle the ship, the pirates he transformed. · A coral-red ground fills the whole interior, sea and sky in one. · Outside, big eyes on each side and warriors fighting over a fallen body. · As the drinker drained the cup, the god seemed to rise out of the red wine. · Kylix · A shallow two-handled drinking cup used at the symposium, the Greek drinking party. · Pirates kidnapped Dionysus, not knowing he was a god. He filled the ship with vines and turned them into dolphins as they leapt overboard."},{"id":"h10_patinir","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h10_patinir.html","h":4,"kind":"work","label":"Charon Crossing the Styx","title":"Charon Crossing the Styx","artist":"Joachim Patinir","thumb":"h10_patinir","text":"Charon Crossing the Styx · Joachim Patinir · 1520–24 · Oil on panel, 64 × 103 cm · Museo del Prado, Madrid · Patinir, a pioneer of landscape painting in Antwerp, turns the river of the dead into a moral choice. It probably belonged to Philip II and was saved from the Alcázar fire of 1734. · The picture divides into three bands: paradise with angels at left, the dark Styx in the middle, hell at right. · Cerberus guards the gate of hell on the right. · The small soul in Charon’s boat turns, in profile, toward the easy road to damnation. · Charon the ferryman carried the souls of the dead across the Styx for a coin. Patinir blends this pagan image with Christian heaven and hell."},{"id":"h10_orpheusrel","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h10_orpheusrel.html","h":4,"kind":"work","label":"Orpheus, Eurydice and Hermes","title":"Orpheus, Eurydice and Hermes","artist":"Roman, after an Athenian relief","thumb":"h10_orpheusrel","text":"Orpheus, Eurydice and Hermes · Roman, after an Athenian relief · 1st century BCE, after an original of the late 5th century BCE · Marble, 121 × 98 cm · Louvre, Paris · A Roman copy, probably made in Italy, of a lost Athenian relief from the end of the 5th century BCE, which may have belonged to the Altar of the Twelve Gods in Athens. A nearly identical copy is in Naples. · Orpheus has just turned to look; Hermes, guide of souls, takes Eurydice’s hand to lead her back. · Orpheus holds his lyre; Hermes wears a traveller’s hat and boots. · A Latin inscription added before the 1500s names the figures wrongly, as Zethus, Antiope and Amphion. · Hermes’s legs and the upper right corner are later restorations. · Formerly in the Borghese collection; bought by the Louvre in 1807. · Orpheus charmed the gods of the underworld with his music and was allowed to lead his dead wife Eurydice home, if he did not look back. He looked, and lost her for ever."},{"id":"h10_corot","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h10_corot.html","h":4,"kind":"work","label":"Orpheus Leading Eurydice from the Underworld","title":"Orpheus Leading Eurydice from the Underworld","artist":"Camille Corot","thumb":"h10_corot","text":"Orpheus Leading Eurydice from the Underworld · Camille Corot · 1861 · Oil on canvas, 112.7 × 137.2 cm · Museum of Fine Arts, Houston · Shown at the Paris Salon of 1861, this is Corot’s soft, misty landscape style applied to myth. The mood foreshadows the ending rather than showing it. · Orpheus leads Eurydice out, before he looks back. · Spirits of the dead gather beneath feathery trees. · A narrow range of grays, greens and blues gives a filtered, dreamlike light. · Signed “COROT” at lower left; bought by the museum in 1987. · Corot paints the moment of hope in the Orpheus story, just before the backward glance."},{"id":"h10_map","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h10_map.html","h":4,"kind":"work","label":"The Map of Hell","title":"The Map of Hell","artist":"Sandro Botticelli","thumb":"h10_map","text":"The Map of Hell · Sandro Botticelli · c. 1480–95 · Silverpoint, pen and ink, and colour on parchment · Vatican Apostolic Library · The opening sheet of Botticelli’s illustrations to Dante’s Divine Comedy, made for Lorenzo di Pierfrancesco de’ Medici. It is one of the few sheets he finished in colour. · Hell is a giant funnel of circular ledges crowded with tiny figures. · Most of the cycle stayed as line drawing; this sheet is coloured. · Bought in 1650 for Queen Christina of Sweden; most of the other sheets are in Berlin. · Dante borrows freely from Greek myth: Charon, Minos and Cerberus all work in his hell. · Dante’s Christian hell keeps the furniture of the classical underworld, which is why it belongs in a guide to myth."},{"id":"h5-0","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/part-5.html","h":5,"kind":"overview","label":"Overview","title":"Heroes","artist":"","thumb":"h11_cellini","text":"Heroes · Perseus, Heracles, Theseus, Oedipus, Jason and Medea · Greek heroes are mortals, often half-divine, who win fame by killing monsters, finishing impossible tasks and outwitting fate. Their stories are also warnings: the same strength and pride that make a hero often destroy him or those he loves. · Monster-slaying gave rulers a ready image of order defeating chaos. · Heracles became a model of virtue chosen over pleasure. · Later artists turned to the darker heroes: Oedipus, Jason and Medea. · Hero · In Greek usage, a figure of the age of legend, often the child of a god, honoured with cult after death. · Apotheosis · Being raised to the status of a god, as Heracles was after his death. · Benvenuto Cellini · Perseus with the Head of Medusa · 1545–54"},{"id":"h11_caravaggio","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h11_caravaggio.html","h":5,"kind":"work","label":"Medusa","title":"Medusa","artist":"Caravaggio","thumb":"h11_caravaggio","text":"Medusa · Caravaggio · c. 1595–98 · Oil on canvas mounted on a wooden shield · Uffizi Gallery, Florence · A real parade shield painted with the severed head of Medusa. Cardinal Francesco Maria del Monte gave it to Grand Duke Ferdinando I de’ Medici in 1598. · Medusa screams with eyes wide, caught between life and death at the moment of beheading. · Snakes writhe around her head and blood streams from the neck. · Kept in the Medici armoury, first without a name; listed as Caravaggio’s in 1631 and shown with a suit of Persian armour. · The Medici used the Medusa head as an emblem of power; the shield also recalls the shield Perseus used to see her safely. · Medusa’s gaze turned people to stone. Perseus cut off her head by watching her reflection in a polished shield, and Athena later set the head on her own shield."},{"id":"h11_cellini","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h11_cellini.html","h":5,"kind":"work","label":"Perseus with the Head of Medusa","title":"Perseus with the Head of Medusa","artist":"Benvenuto Cellini","thumb":"h11_cellini","text":"Perseus with the Head of Medusa · Benvenuto Cellini · 1545–54 · Bronze, statue over 3 m high · Loggia dei Lanzi, Florence · Commissioned by Duke Cosimo I de’ Medici in 1545 for the Loggia dei Lanzi on Florence’s main square, beside Donatello’s Judith. It was unveiled in April 1554 and read as the triumph of the duke over the old republic. · Cast in separate pieces and joined; the dramatic pour Cellini describes in his autobiography was the Perseus figure. · Blood pours from Medusa’s neck in bronze; Perseus holds the head high. · Cellini signed it on the strap across Perseus’s chest. · The original marble base and its statuettes are now in the Bargello; copies stand in the Loggia. · Perseus, son of Zeus and Danaë, killed Medusa with gifts from the gods: winged sandals, a cap of invisibility and a curved sword."},{"id":"h11_titian","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h11_titian.html","h":5,"kind":"work","label":"Perseus and Andromeda","title":"Perseus and Andromeda","artist":"Titian","thumb":"h11_titian","text":"Perseus and Andromeda · Titian · probably 1554–56 · Oil on canvas, 173 × 186.8 cm · Wallace Collection, London · One of Titian’s six poesie for Philip II, conceived as a partner to The Rape of Europa. It later passed through the collections of Van Dyck and the Orléans family before Lady Wallace left it to the nation in 1897. · Andromeda’s pale body, chained to the rock, stretches across the canvas. · Perseus dives at the sea monster over the water. · Its condition has suffered since the 16th century. · Reunited with the other poesie in 2020–21 in London, Madrid and Boston. · Queen Cassiopeia boasted that she and her daughter outshone the sea nymphs, and a monster was sent to ravage the coast. Perseus freed Andromeda from the rock and married her."},{"id":"h12_farnese","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h12_farnese.html","h":5,"kind":"work","label":"Farnese Hercules","title":"Farnese Hercules","artist":"Glykon of Athens, after Lysippos","thumb":"h12_farnese","text":"Farnese Hercules · Glykon of Athens, after Lysippos · 2nd–3rd century CE · Pentelic marble, 317 cm high · National Archaeological Museum, Naples · An enlarged Roman copy, signed by Glykon of Athens, of a 4th-century BCE bronze attributed to Lysippos. It was found in 1546 in the Baths of Caracalla in Rome, where it was probably displayed. · A colossal, heavily muscled hero leans on his club, small head bowed. · His right hand, behind his back, holds the golden apples of the Hesperides, his last labour. · It entered the Farnese collection and moved with it to Naples. · The photograph shown is by Giorgio Sommer, about the 1860s. · Heracles (Roman Hercules), son of Zeus, performed twelve labours to atone for killing his family in a madness sent by Hera."},{"id":"h12_crossroads","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h12_crossroads.html","h":5,"kind":"work","label":"The Choice of Hercules","title":"The Choice of Hercules","artist":"Annibale Carracci","thumb":"h12_crossroads","text":"The Choice of Hercules · Annibale Carracci · 1596 · Oil on canvas · Museo di Capodimonte, Naples · The central ceiling painting of the Camerino, a small private study in Palazzo Farnese in Rome that Annibale decorated in 1595–97 for Cardinal Odoardo Farnese. It was later removed and taken to Parma, then to Naples. · Pleasure, at right, points to cards, music and theatre masks in a green, flowery setting. · Virtue, at left, points up a hard, rocky path toward Pegasus. · A laurel-crowned poet waits to record the hero’s fame. · The programme was devised by the palace librarian, Fulvio Orsini. · In a Greek parable told by Prodicus and retold by Cicero, the young Hercules meets Virtue and Pleasure at a fork in the road and chooses the harder way."},{"id":"h12_antaios","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h12_antaios.html","h":5,"kind":"work","label":"Krater with Heracles Wrestling Antaeus","title":"Krater with Heracles Wrestling Antaeus","artist":"Euphronios","thumb":"h12_antaios","text":"Krater with Heracles Wrestling Antaeus · Euphronios · c. 515–510 BCE · Red-figure terracotta, 45.5 cm high · Louvre, Paris · A wine-mixing bowl signed by Euphronios, one of the first great red-figure painters, made in Athens and found in Etruria. The Louvre bought it with the Campana collection in 1861. · Heracles kneels and strangles Antaeus, who is half-lying on the ground. · Compare the faces: Antaeus’s eyes roll back and his teeth show; Heracles stays calm. · Heracles’s neat curls are built from tiny raised dots of clay. · The scene appeared on a Greek stamp for the 1906 Athens games. · Red-figure · A vase technique, invented in Athens about 530 BCE, in which figures are left in the red clay and the background painted black. · Krater · A large bowl for mixing wine with water. · The giant Antaeus, son of Poseidon and Earth, drew strength from touching the ground. In the poems Heracles lifted him into the air to defeat him; the painter shows a different moment."},{"id":"h13_theseusvase","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h13_theseusvase.html","h":5,"kind":"work","label":"Amphora with Theseus Killing the Minotaur","title":"Amphora with Theseus Killing the Minotaur","artist":"Group of London B 174","thumb":"h13_theseusvase","text":"Amphora with Theseus Killing the Minotaur · Group of London B 174 · c. 540–530 BCE · Black-figure terracotta, 42.3 cm high · Louvre, Paris · An Athenian black-figure amphora found at Vulci in Etruria, in excavations by Lucien Bonaparte, prince of Canino. The Louvre bought it in 1840. · Theseus, bearded and in an embroidered tunic, drives his sword into the wounded, half-kneeling Minotaur. · Look for the small bird in the field between them. · Two young women and two young men watch, the Athenian tribute. · The other side shows Heracles wrestling the Nemean lion. · Parts of the black surface were repainted in modern times. · Each year Athens sent young people to Crete to be eaten by the Minotaur in the Labyrinth. Theseus killed the monster and found his way out with Ariadne’s thread."},{"id":"h13_icarus","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h13_icarus.html","h":5,"kind":"work","label":"Landscape with the Fall of Icarus","title":"Landscape with the Fall of Icarus","artist":"After Pieter Bruegel the Elder","thumb":"h13_icarus","text":"Landscape with the Fall of Icarus · After Pieter Bruegel the Elder · c. 1600, after an original of c. 1560 · Oil on canvas, 73.5 × 112 cm · Royal Museums of Fine Arts of Belgium, Brussels · The design is Bruegel’s, but a 2011 technical study concluded this canvas is a copy by another hand, painted around 1600 after a lost original. It is still the best-known record of his composition. · Bought in 1912 as a copy; for decades some argued it was an original. · The underdrawing was judged weak and hesitant, a copyist’s work. · Look for Icarus’s legs in the water near the ship at lower right, while the ploughman works on. · Another version is in the Van Buuren Museum, Brussels. · Daedalus made wings of feathers and wax for himself and his son Icarus. Icarus flew too near the sun, the wax melted, and he fell into the sea."},{"id":"h14_ingres","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h14_ingres.html","h":5,"kind":"work","label":"Oedipus Explains the Riddle of the Sphinx","title":"Oedipus Explains the Riddle of the Sphinx","artist":"Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres","thumb":"h14_ingres","text":"Oedipus Explains the Riddle of the Sphinx · Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres · 1808, enlarged 1827 · Oil on canvas, 189 × 144 cm · Louvre, Paris · Painted in Rome as a required work for the French Academy while Ingres was a student at the Villa Medici. He enlarged the canvas in 1827 and showed it at the Salon that year. · Oedipus, nude and holding a spear, leans toward the Sphinx on her rock as he answers. · Bones of earlier victims lie among the rocks. · Signed and dated 1808 at lower left. · Owned by the duc d’Orléans; bequeathed to the Louvre in 1878. · The Sphinx killed travellers who could not answer her riddle. Oedipus solved it and became king of Thebes, unaware he had already killed his father and would marry his mother."},{"id":"h14_jason","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h14_jason.html","h":5,"kind":"work","label":"Jason","title":"Jason","artist":"Gustave Moreau","thumb":"h14_jason","text":"Jason · Gustave Moreau · 1865 · Oil on canvas, 204 × 115.5 cm · Musée d’Orsay, Paris · Shown at the Salon of 1865, a year after Moreau’s Oedipus made his name. Its dense, jewel-like detail is typical of his approach to myth. · Jason stands with his foot on the dead guardian, painted as an eagle rather than a dragon. · Medea stands behind him holding a small vial. · A column with a ram’s head stands for the Golden Fleece. · Given to the French state by Théodore Reinach in 1908. · Jason sailed with the Argonauts to Colchis to win the Golden Fleece. He succeeded only with the help of the king’s daughter, the sorceress Medea."},{"id":"h14_medea","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h14_medea.html","h":5,"kind":"work","label":"Medea about to Kill Her Children","title":"Medea about to Kill Her Children","artist":"Eugène Delacroix","thumb":"h14_medea","text":"Medea about to Kill Her Children · Eugène Delacroix · 1838 · Oil on canvas, 260 × 165 cm · Palais des Beaux-Arts, Lille · A critical success at the Salon of 1838, where the French state bought it; it has been in Lille since about 1840. Lille also has the oil sketch and about thirty preparatory drawings. · Medea clutches her two struggling sons in a cave, the group forming a pyramid. · She glances back as if she hears danger; a shadow falls across her face like a mask. · The dagger, the only upright line in the picture, is set off by white cloth. · Delacroix painted a smaller replica in 1862, now in the Louvre. · When Jason abandoned her to marry a princess, Medea took revenge by killing their children. Euripides made the story a tragedy in 431 BCE."},{"id":"h6-0","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/part-6.html","h":6,"kind":"overview","label":"Overview","title":"Troy and the Odyssey","artist":"","thumb":"h16_turner","text":"Troy and the Odyssey · The war over Helen, and Odysseus’s long voyage home · The Trojan War began with a wedding, a golden apple and a beauty contest, and ended ten years later with a wooden horse. Homer’s Iliad tells one episode of the war; his Odyssey follows the hero Odysseus on his ten-year journey home. · Greek vase painters told the Trojan stories in close, labelled detail. · Later painters chose the moments of grief and fate: Hector’s death, Laocoön, the Cyclops. · Odysseus’s wit, not strength, carries him through monsters and enchantresses. · Epic · A long narrative poem about heroes and gods, such as the Iliad and the Odyssey. · J. M. W. Turner · Ulysses Deriding Polyphemus · 1829"},{"id":"h15_francois","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h15_francois.html","h":6,"kind":"work","label":"The François Vase","title":"The François Vase","artist":"Kleitias (painter) and Ergotimos (potter)","thumb":"h15_francois","text":"The François Vase · Kleitias (painter) and Ergotimos (potter) · c. 570–560 BCE · Black-figure terracotta volute krater, 66 cm high · Museo Archeologico Nazionale, Florence · A huge wine-mixing bowl signed by both its painter and its potter, carrying about 270 figures and 121 painted name labels. It was found in pieces in an Etruscan tomb near Chiusi in the 1840s. · The main band shows the gods arriving at the wedding of Peleus and Thetis, the parents of Achilles. · Dionysus carries a wine jar and turns his face to the viewer, rare at this date. · On each handle, Ajax carries the dead Achilles. · In 1900 a museum guard smashed it into 638 pieces; it has been reassembled twice. · Volute krater · A krater whose handles curl into scrolls (volutes) above the rim. · The goddess Eris, left off the guest list for this wedding, threw a golden apple “for the fairest” among the guests, which led to the Judgement of Paris and the Trojan War."},{"id":"h15_exekias","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h15_exekias.html","h":6,"kind":"work","label":"Achilles and Ajax Playing a Game","title":"Achilles and Ajax Playing a Game","artist":"Exekias","thumb":"h15_exekias","text":"Achilles and Ajax Playing a Game · Exekias · c. 540–530 BCE · Black-figure terracotta amphora, 61.1 cm high · Vatican Museums, Vatican City · Exekias signed this amphora as both potter and painter. Its scene, found nowhere in Homer, shows the two greatest Greek warriors bent over a board game during the war. It was found at Vulci in Etruria and given to Pope Gregory XVI in 1834. · Words come from their mouths: “four” for Achilles, “three” for Ajax, so Achilles is winning. · Achilles still wears his helmet; Ajax has taken his off. · Very fine incised detail covers their cloaks. · On the other side, Castor and Pollux return home to their parents. · Achilles and Ajax were the strongest Greek fighters at Troy. Both died there: Achilles by Paris’s arrow, Ajax by his own hand."},{"id":"h15_paris","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h15_paris.html","h":6,"kind":"work","label":"The Judgement of Paris","title":"The Judgement of Paris","artist":"Peter Paul Rubens","thumb":"h15_paris","text":"The Judgement of Paris · Peter Paul Rubens · probably 1632–35 · Oil on oak panel, 144.8 × 193.7 cm · National Gallery, London · A late Rubens that he reworked and enlarged himself, then another artist altered in France between about 1676 and 1727. The later hand toned it down: removing putti and satyrs and making Paris point the apple at Venus. · Venus, in the centre, receives the apple, with Cupid behind her. · Minerva’s owl sits in the tree; Juno’s peacock hisses at Paris’s dog. · The Fury Alecto lurks in the clouds, foretelling the war. · Bought by the National Gallery in 1844. · Paris, a Trojan prince, judged which goddess was fairest. Juno offered power and wealth, Minerva wisdom, and Venus the most beautiful woman alive, Helen, whose abduction started the war."},{"id":"h15_andromache","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h15_andromache.html","h":6,"kind":"work","label":"Andromache Mourning Hector","title":"Andromache Mourning Hector","artist":"Jacques-Louis David","thumb":"h15_andromache","text":"Andromache Mourning Hector · Jacques-Louis David · 1783 · Oil on canvas, 275 × 203 cm · Louvre, Paris (on deposit from the École des Beaux-Arts) · David’s reception piece for the French Royal Academy, the painting that won him membership, shown at the Salon of 1783. It belongs to the École des Beaux-Arts and was restored in 2025. · Andromache sits beside Hector’s body with their young son Astyanax. · Hector’s helmet and weapons are laid beside him. · The candelabrum at right carries a Greek inscription quoting Andromache’s lament from the Iliad. · Bequeathed to the École des Beaux-Arts in 1886; on deposit at the Louvre since 1969. · Hector, Troy’s greatest defender, was killed by Achilles. The Iliad ends with his funeral and the lament of his wife Andromache."},{"id":"h15_laocoon","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h15_laocoon.html","h":6,"kind":"work","label":"Laocoön","title":"Laocoön","artist":"El Greco","thumb":"h15_laocoon","text":"Laocoön · El Greco · c. 1610–14 · Oil on canvas, 137.5 × 172.5 cm · National Gallery of Art, Washington · El Greco’s only known mythological painting, made in his last years and probably still in his studio when he died. He set the story outside Toledo, the city where he lived. · Elongated nude figures wrestle snakes on a rocky bank. · A tiny horse trots toward a city of pink walls: Toledo standing in for Troy. · The figures at right are unfinished; a 1955 cleaning revealed an extra head and leg. · Bought by the Kress Foundation and given to the gallery in 1946. · The Trojan priest Laocoön warned against the Greeks’ wooden horse. Sea serpents sent by the gods killed him and his sons, and the Trojans took the horse inside."},{"id":"h16_turner","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h16_turner.html","h":6,"kind":"work","label":"Ulysses Deriding Polyphemus","title":"Ulysses Deriding Polyphemus","artist":"J. M. W. Turner","thumb":"h16_turner","text":"Ulysses Deriding Polyphemus · J. M. W. Turner · 1829 · Oil on canvas, 132.5 × 203 cm · National Gallery, London · Painted for the Royal Academy exhibition of 1829, Turner’s Homer is mostly light and colour. It came to the nation with the Turner Bequest in 1856. · Ulysses stands on deck in a scarlet cloak, arms raised, under a red banner. · The giant’s shadowy body sprawls along the clifftop, one hand raised to throw a rock. · The rising sun is drawn by Apollo’s horses; sea nymphs with stars on their foreheads swim at the prow. · Still in its original 19th-century frame. · Odysseus blinded the Cyclops Polyphemus to escape his cave, then taunted him from the ship. Polyphemus prayed to his father Poseidon, who kept Odysseus from home for years."},{"id":"h16_circe","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h16_circe.html","h":6,"kind":"work","label":"Circe Offering the Cup to Ulysses","title":"Circe Offering the Cup to Ulysses","artist":"John William Waterhouse","thumb":"h16_circe","text":"Circe Offering the Cup to Ulysses · John William Waterhouse · 1891 · Oil on canvas, 148 × 92 cm · Gallery Oldham · Bought from Waterhouse in 1892 by the Oldham collector Charles Edward Lees, whose daughter gave it to the town’s gallery in 1952. Waterhouse puts the viewer in Ulysses’s place. · Circe raises a goblet of potion in one hand and her wand in the other. · The round mirror behind her shows Ulysses approaching, from where we stand. · A letter from Waterhouse to Lees survives in the gallery’s archive. · The enchantress Circe turned Odysseus’s men into pigs. Protected by an herb from Hermes, Odysseus resisted her drugs and made her restore them."},{"id":"h7-0","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/part-7.html","h":7,"kind":"overview","label":"Overview","title":"Interpreting Myth","artist":"","thumb":"h18_helios","text":"Interpreting Myth · How later ages read the old stories as allegory and symbol · Every age reads the myths for its own purposes. Renaissance thinkers found hidden philosophy in them, Baroque patrons used them to flatter, and the Symbolists of the 1800s turned them into private dreams. · A myth can carry moral, political or spiritual meaning beyond its plot. · The same story can be told very differently: compare the two versions of Oedipus. · Many meanings are still argued, which is part of the pleasure. · Allegory · A picture or story in which figures stand for ideas. · Symbolism · A late-19th-century movement that used myth and dream to suggest inner states rather than describe the world. · Guido Reni · Aurora · 1613–14"},{"id":"h18_primavera","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h18_primavera.html","h":7,"kind":"work","label":"Primavera","title":"Primavera","artist":"Sandro Botticelli","thumb":"h18_primavera","text":"Primavera · Sandro Botticelli · c. 1480 · Tempera on poplar panel, 207 × 319 cm · Uffizi Gallery, Florence · Recorded in the Florence house of the heirs of Lorenzo di Pierfrancesco de’ Medici, hung above a lettuccio, a carved daybed. The Uffizi says its exact meaning remains a mystery. · At right, the wind Zephyr seizes the nymph Chloris, who reappears transformed as Flora. · Venus stands in the centre; a blindfolded Cupid shoots above her. · At left, Mercury touches the clouds with his staff. · At least 42 plant species can be identified in the meadow. · Since June 2026 it faces The Birth of Venus across the renovated Botticelli rooms. · Many scholars read the painting through the Neoplatonic philosophy of Marsilio Ficino, as an image of love rising from the physical to the spiritual."},{"id":"h18_helios","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h18_helios.html","h":7,"kind":"work","label":"Aurora","title":"Aurora","artist":"Guido Reni","thumb":"h18_helios","text":"Aurora · Guido Reni · 1613–14 · Fresco · Casino dell’Aurora Pallavicini, Rome · Painted on the ceiling of a garden pavilion built in 1612–13 for Cardinal Scipione Borghese as a summer retreat. It is still in private hands and opens to visitors on the first day of most months. · Veiled Aurora, the dawn, flies ahead scattering flowers. · Apollo drives the sun’s four-horse chariot, ringed by the Hours. · Phosphorus, the morning star, flies among the figures. · Most recently restored and unveiled in 2022. · Each morning Aurora opens the gates of heaven for the sun. For a cardinal’s summer house, the daily return of light became an image of joy and renewal."},{"id":"h18_moreau","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/h18_moreau.html","h":7,"kind":"work","label":"Oedipus and the Sphinx","title":"Oedipus and the Sphinx","artist":"Gustave Moreau","thumb":"h18_moreau","text":"Oedipus and the Sphinx · Gustave Moreau · 1864 · Oil on canvas, 206.4 × 104.8 cm · Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · The painting that made Moreau’s name at the Paris Salon of 1864, and a major influence on later Symbolist artists and writers such as Odilon Redon and Oscar Wilde. Compare it with Ingres’s calm version of 1808. · The Sphinx, woman-headed with blue wings, lion claws and a serpent tail, confronts Oedipus at close quarters. · Remains of earlier victims lie at the bottom: a hand, a foot, a crown. · An ornate urn sits on the column at right. · Bequeathed to the Met in 1920. · Moreau turns Ingres’s contest of reason into a tense standoff between man and monster, the Symbolist reading of the same myth."},{"id":"sources","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/sources.html","h":99,"kind":"sources","label":"Sources","title":"Sources","artist":"","thumb":null,"text":"Sources"},{"id":"credits","url":"/study/art-history/classical-myth-v2-proto/credits.html","h":99,"kind":"credits","label":"Image credits","title":"Image credits","artist":"","thumb":null,"text":"Image credits"}]};
